<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willibald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700–<a href="/years/787">787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Willibald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willibald was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> monk and pilgrim, a kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>. He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in the 720s, the first recorded English pilgrim there, and spent ten years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. Boniface made him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> around 741.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willibald.jpg" alt="Willibald" width="320" height="751" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rensi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_Willibald_Handschuher-Figur_Spitalkirche_Eichst%C3%A4tt.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
